<%- include('partials/header', { title: title }) %>

<%# Focus starts on the heading when there is a failure to explain. %>
<h1 tabindex="-1" <%= error ? 'data-autofocus' : '' %>>Lost your device</h1>

<% if (error) { %>
  <p class="status" data-tone="error"><%= error %></p>
<% } %>

<p>
  If every device that holds one of your passkeys is lost, broken or stolen,
  you can replace them using a recovery code.
</p>

<%# recover.js is shared with /recover/status, where it does announce through
    #status; this page's own form is a plain full-page POST that never
    touches it, but the live region is included anyway, matching every other
    page that loads a /js/ script, so the shared script always has somewhere
    safe to write if that ever changes. %>
<%- include('partials/status') %>

<h2>What happens after you enter a code</h2>
<%#
  Set out in full, before the user commits to anything. A 24 hour wait is a
  surprise worth avoiding, and someone who is locked out needs to know now
  whether this route will get them back in time.
%>
<ol>
  <li>The code is used up. Each one works only once.</li>
  <li>
    You are signed in, but <strong>restricted</strong>: you can look at your
    account, and nothing else. You cannot add passkeys, change recovery codes
    or turn devices off.
  </li>
  <li>
    A <strong>24 hour wait</strong> begins, and we tell you on your other
    contact channels that this is happening.
  </li>
  <li>
    During the wait, anyone who can sign in normally can cancel it — so if it
    was not you, the real owner can stop it.
  </li>
  <li>
    After the wait, you register a new passkey, and every old passkey on the
    account is turned off.
  </li>
</ol>

<h2>Enter your recovery code</h2>

<form method="post" action="/recover">
  <div class="field">
    <label for="username">Username</label>
    <input
      type="text"
      id="username"
      name="username"
      value="<%= username %>"
      autocapitalize="none"
      spellcheck="false"
      required
    />
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="code">Recovery code</label>
    <p class="hint" id="code-hint">
      Ten characters, for example ABCDE-FGHJK. Dashes, spaces and lower case
      are all fine.
    </p>
    <%#
      autocomplete="off" and no type="password": a recovery code being typed
      by someone using a screen reader needs to be readable back to them, and
      masking it only makes a hard task harder. It is not a reusable secret.
    %>
    <input
      type="text"
      id="code"
      name="code"
      aria-describedby="code-hint"
      autocomplete="off"
      autocapitalize="characters"
      spellcheck="false"
      required
    />
  </div>

  <p><button type="submit">Start replacing my passkeys</button></p>
</form>

<p>
  Out of recovery codes? You will need to contact support and prove who you
  are another way. There is no route through this page without one.
</p>

<p><a href="/signin">Back to signing in</a></p>

<%- include('partials/footer', { script: '/js/recover.js' }) %>
